   "html_text": "Although Monroe Beardsley (1982) often spoke positively of Dewey’s notion of aesthetic experience, he thought that Dewey was obsessed with the dangers of dualism and that he talked about “separation” in a misleading way. Dewey thinks the practices of hanging paintings in special buildings would deny continuity between art and life. Yet Beardsley sees no real problem here, for people who see a painting in a museum bring their culture with them. Also, against Dewey’s stress on continuity, Beardsle",
   "md_text": "Although Monroe Beardsley (1982) often spoke positively of Dewey’s notion of aesthetic experience, he thought that Dewey was obsessed with the dangers of dualism and that he talked about “separation” in a misleading way. Dewey thinks the practices of hanging paintings in special buildings would deny continuity between art and life. Yet Beardsley sees no real problem here, for people who see a painting in a museum bring their culture with them. Also, against Dewey’s stress on continuity, Beardsle"
